{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017FZzT4fFUkqjXJtmu2tr5C","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Review: Migrate Hankweave to Monorepo\n     2→\n     3→Source plan: `plan/merged/plan.md`\n     4→\n     5→This review reads the plan with fresh eyes against the actual source code. Notes are grouped by the section of the plan they relate to. Each item describes what was observed and why it matters.\n     6→\n     7→---\n     8→\n     9→## 1. Line-number claims that are wrong or misleading\n    10→\n    11→The plan references specific line numbers for several files. After reading the actual source, several are incorrect.\n    12→\n    13→**`sync-public.ts` — `STRIP_DIRS` is at line 28 (correct), but the plan's proposed change is incomplete.**\n    14→\n    15→The plan (Step 3.1) says to add `\"docs\"` to `STRIP_DIRS`. Looking at the actual `prepareSnapshot()` function (`sync-public.ts:214`), the rsync excludes are built as:\n    16→\n    17→```typescript\n    18→const dirExcludes = [\".git\", ...STRIP_DIRS].map((p) => `--exclude='${p}/'`);\n    19→```\n    20→\n    21→This adds a trailing slash, making the rsync exclude `--exclude='docs/'`. That is correct for rsync behavior (excludes the directory but not a file named `docs`). No issue here, but the plan doesn't surface this detail. The implementing agent should understand that STRIP_DIRS entries become dir-style excludes with trailing slashes in the rsync call.\n    22→\n    23→**`sync-public.ts` — `transformPackageJson()` is at lines 173–196 (the plan says this).** After reading the code, the function runs at line 173 and ends at line 196. The plan's proposed diff (Step 3.2) would insert `delete pkg.workspaces;` after `pkg.homepage = ...`. This is correct. However, the plan quotes the full function in its proposed diff but omits the console.log lines at 189–195. The implementing agent should make a minimal targeted edit, not rewrite the entire function. This is a clarification, not a blocking issue.\n    24→\n    25→**`release.ts` — `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP` is at lines 54–60.** Confirmed correct.\n    26→\n    27→**`release.ts` — \"schema revert at around line 436.\"** The actual code at lines 424–445 shows the `git checkout -- schemas/` call is at line 436. This is accurate.\n    28→\n    29→**`release.ts` — \"release commit `git add` at line 510.\"** The actual code at lines 509–511 is:\n    30→```typescript\n    31→await exec(\"git add package.json CHANGELOG.md schemas/\");\n    32→await exec(`git commit -m \"Release v${newVersion}\"`);\n    33→```\n    34→The `git add` is indeed at line 510. Correct.\n    35→\n    36→**`test-sync-local.ts` — \"rsync at line 80.\"** The actual rsync call is at line 80:\n    37→```\n    38→rsync -av --exclude='.git' --exclude='node_modules' --exclude='intermediates' ${REPO_ROOT}/ private/\n    39→```\n    40→Correct.\n    41→\n    42→**`test-sync-local.ts` — \"`shouldNotExist` at line 160.\"** The actual `shouldNotExist` array starts at line 160. Confirmed.\n    43→\n    44→**`generate-schemas.ts` — `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` at lines 24–32.** Confirmed correct. The `docsAvailable` check is at line 76. Confirmed correct.\n    45→\n    46→---\n    47→\n    48→## 2. The `generate-schemas.ts` `docsAvailable` guard: plan recommends keeping it but misses a real subtlety\n    49→\n    50→The plan (Step 2.2) says:\n    51→\n    52→> \"Keep the check for defensive safety — it doesn't hurt.\"\n    53→\n    54→The actual guard at line 76 is:\n    55→\n    56→```typescript\n    57→const docsAvailable = fs.existsSync(path.dirname(DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR));\n    58→```\n    59→\n    60→`path.dirname(DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR)` in the post-migration world resolves to `docs/public`. This is the parent of the `schemas/` subdirectory inside `docs/public`. This means the guard checks whether `docs/public/` exists — not whether `docs/` exists. This is a fine distinction but matters: if someone were to run the script on a fresh checkout where `docs/public/` exists but `docs/public/schemas/` does not, the `mkdirSync` on line 78 creates it. That's correct behavior.\n    61→\n    62→The plan's proposed comment update says: \"Always available in monorepo; guard kept for safety in case `docs/` is missing from checkout.\" This comment is slightly imprecise — the guard checks `docs/public/`, not `docs/`. The implementing agent should write the updated comment more precisely.\n    63→\n    64→---\n    65→\n    66→## 3. The `.gitignore` changes: the plan is partly wrong\n    67→\n    68→The plan (Step 4.1) says:\n    69→\n    70→> \"The root `.gitignore` already has a generic `.next` and `out` entry (lines 141–142: `.next` and `out`), but since those are not scoped to `docs/`, they'd match those directories anywhere in the tree including at root.\"\n    71→\n    72→This is accurate. What the plan then says — \"The `docs/`-prefixed versions are more explicit but the existing generic entries already cover the docs case\" — is also accurate, but the recommendation to only add `docs/public/_pagefind/` is incomplete.\n    73→\n    74→Reading the actual `.gitignore`, the entry at line 147 is `dist`, not scoped to any subdirectory. The runtime's build outputs go into `releases/` (line 16 of the gitignore), not `dist/`. The `dist` entry in `.gitignore` therefore won't match the runtime build because the runtime uses `releases/` as its output directory. However, the `dist` entry *would* match `docs/dist/` if Next.js ever emits to `docs/dist/`. In practice, `next build` outputs to `.next/` and static export to `out/`. This entry is a non-issue for the actual migration.\n    75→\n    76→The plan should clarify one item it misses: the docs workspace has a `bun.lock` that will be deleted (Step 1.3). That file won't be left around. But the `.next/` and `out` patterns at `.gitignore` lines 141–142 are *unscoped* and at root level. In gitignore syntax, a pattern without a leading `/` or `/` in the middle matches anywhere in the tree. So `.next` matches `docs/.next/` automatically. The plan is correct that the existing entries cover the docs case, but it should explicitly state this is because the patterns are tree-wide rather than hinting it's a coincidence.\n    77→\n    78→---\n    79→\n    80→## 4. The `sync-public.ts` `initialSync()` function also needs updating — the plan misses this\n    81→\n    82→The plan (Phase 3) updates `STRIP_DIRS` to add `\"docs\"`. This correctly covers `prepareSnapshot()` and `snapshotSync()`. However, `initialSync()` at line 262 has its own explicit loop:\n    83→\n    84→```typescript\n    85→// Remove internal files\n    86→for (const dir of STRIP_DIRS) {\n    87→  const fullPath = join(publicDir, dir);\n    88→  if (existsSync(fullPath)) {\n    89→    await rm(fullPath, { recursive: true, force: true });\n    90→  }\n    91→}\n    92→```\n    93→\n    94→Since `STRIP_DIRS` is a shared constant and `initialSync()` iterates the same constant, adding `\"docs\"` to `STRIP_DIRS` will cover `initialSync()` automatically. The plan does not explicitly mention this, which could confuse the implementing agent into thinking only `prepareSnapshot()` is affected. The plan should acknowledge that `initialSync()` is also covered by the `STRIP_DIRS` change because it uses the same constant.\n    95→\n    96→---\n    97→\n    98→## 5. The release script's schema handling: the plan's proposed code change has an issue\n    99→\n   100→The plan (Step 3.4) proposes extending the validation-phase schema revert to:\n   101→\n   102→```typescript\n   103→await exec(\"git checkout -- schemas/ docs/public/schemas/ 2>/dev/null || true\", {\n   104→  silent: true,\n   105→  allowFailure: true,\n   106→});\n   107→```\n   108→\n   109→Reading the actual code at lines 435–439:\n   110→\n   111→```typescript\n   112→await exec(\"git checkout -- schemas/ 2>/dev/null || true\", {\n   113→  silent: true,\n   114→  allowFailure: true,\n   115→});\n   116→```\n   117→\n   118→The existing call uses `allowFailure: true`. The plan's proposed change correctly maintains `allowFailure: true`. No issue there. But there is a subtle problem: if `docs/public/schemas/` does not yet exist in the git index (fresh checkout, schemas not yet generated), `git checkout -- docs/public/schemas/` will fail with \"error: pathspec 'docs/public/schemas/' did not match any file(s) known to git.\" The `2>/dev/null || true` suffix in the shell command handles this at the shell level. However, the actual `exec()` helper checks exit code and only suppresses the throw if `allowFailure: true`. Since `allowFailure: true` is set, this is handled correctly.\n   119→\n   120→The more important issue is different: the plan says \"without this, the validation phase leaves `docs/public/schemas/` in a modified-but-unstaged state.\" That is correct, but there is another consequence: `bun run tc` (invoked during validation at line 451) runs *after* schema generation at line 425. If schema generation modifies files in `docs/public/schemas/` and the revert at line 436 only covers `schemas/`, the `git status --porcelain` check at line 210 (in the next release run, not this one) would show uncommitted changes. The plan is right to fix this — just surfacing the precise reason.\n   121→\n   122→---\n   123→\n   124→## 6. The `release.ts` strip step: `docs/` vs `docs` in `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP`\n   125→\n   126→The plan (Step 3.3) proposes adding `\"docs/\"` (with trailing slash) to `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP`. The existing entries use trailing slashes for directories (`\"intermediates/\"`, `\"external-docs/\"`) and no trailing slash for files (`\"CLAUDE.md\"`).\n   127→\n   128→The merge conflict auto-resolution logic at line 380 checks `f.startsWith(s)`. Git reports paths like `docs/content/guides/getting-started.mdx`. The plan correctly notes that `\"docs/content/guides/getting-started.mdx\".startsWith(\"docs/\")` is `true`. This is correct.\n   129→\n   130→However, the strip execution at lines 592–607 uses:\n   131→```typescript\n   132→const exists = await exec(`git ls-files ${file} | head -n 1`, { silent: true });\n   133→```\n   134→This passes `\"docs/\"` (with trailing slash) as the git ls-files argument. `git ls-files docs/` correctly lists files under `docs/`, so trailing slash is fine for the ls-files call.\n   135→\n   136→Then at line 600: `await exec(\\`git rm -rf ${filesToRemove.join(\" \")}\\`)`. This passes `docs/` with trailing slash to `git rm -rf`. In git, `git rm -rf docs/` is equivalent to `git rm -rf docs` — both remove the directory recursively. No issue.\n   137→\n   138→This is consistent with the existing entries. No action needed, but the plan could mention it.\n   139→\n   140→---\n   141→\n   142→## 7. The `test-sync-local.ts` update: plan is missing coverage for the `--initial` mode path\n   143→\n   144→The plan (Step 3.5) adds `--exclude='docs'` to the `--use-head` rsync. However, looking at the actual code, the `--use-head` path is the only one that does an rsync from the live working directory. The other branch (lines 84–86) does a `git clone --branch v${version}`, which naturally excludes uncommitted `docs/` content (since `docs/` would be committed). The `--exclude='docs'` addition is therefore only needed in the `--use-head` path, which is what the plan proposes. This is correct.\n   145→\n   146→One thing the plan does not address: in the `--use-head` path, after the rsync, the code does `git init && git add -A && git commit -m \"Test snapshot\"`. After migration, `docs/` exists at the repo root. If `--exclude='docs'` is added to rsync, then `docs/` won't be in the test snapshot — which is the desired behavior. This is consistent and correct. The plan is fine here.\n   147→\n   148→---\n   149→\n   150→## 8. The `package.json` `\"workspaces\"` field: unclear ordering instruction\n   151→\n   152→The plan (Step 1.2) says to place `\"workspaces\"` \"after `\\\"engines\\\"` and before `\\\"scripts\\\"`.\" Looking at the actual `package.json`, the field order is:\n   153→\n   154→```\n   155→name, version, description, author, keywords, homepage, type, publishConfig,\n   156→bin, main, files, engines, scripts, devDependencies, peerDependencies, dependencies\n   157→```\n   158→\n   159→So `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` between `\"engines\"` and `\"scripts\"` is clear. This is correct and reasonable. No issue.\n   160→\n   161→However, the plan does not explicitly state what the minimal diff looks like. The plan only shows a fragment with `...` placeholders. For an implementing agent working without access to the full file, this could be ambiguous. The actual edit is just adding one line. It would be helpful for the plan to state this plainly: \"Add a single line `\\\"workspaces\\\": [\\\"docs\\\"],` between the closing `}` of `engines` and the opening `{` of `scripts`.\"\n   162→\n   163→---\n   164→\n   165→## 9. `vercel.json` CVE flag: this needs explicit team confirmation, not just a note\n   166→\n   167→The plan (Step 7.1) mentions:\n   168→\n   169→> \"This env var flag is being carried over verbatim from the existing docs repo and is assumed to be intentional for the docs deployment. The implementing agent should confirm this is expected.\"\n   170→\n   171→The actual `vercel.json` contains:\n   172→\n   173→```json\n   174→\"build\": {\n   175→  \"env\": {\n   176→    \"DANGEROUSLY_DEPLOY_VULNERABLE_CVE_2026_0969\": \"1\"\n   177→  }\n   178→}\n   179→```\n   180→\n   181→This is a Vercel-specific mechanism to acknowledge deploying with a known CVE. The plan correctly notes it and flags it for confirmation, but frames it as something the implementing agent should \"confirm.\" In practice, this flag was deliberately set in the existing docs repo — it was a conscious team decision. The implementing agent cannot \"confirm\" this on their own; it needs an explicit go-ahead from a human who owns the docs deployment. The plan should be clearer that this is a **human-approval gate**, not just a code check. The implementing agent should not proceed with Phase 7 without explicit sign-off from a team member who knows why this flag exists.\n   182→\n   183→---\n   184→\n   185→## 10. Phase 7 Vercel: the `installCommand` in `vercel.json` creates a workspace resolution problem\n   186→\n   187→The plan (Step 7.2) identifies this correctly:\n   188→\n   189→> \"The `vercel.json` `installCommand` is `\\\"bun install\\\"`, which runs from the Vercel-configured root directory (`docs/`). Because `docs/` is a workspace member, Bun should look upward for the workspace root's `package.json` and install the full workspace dependency tree. However, if Vercel only mounts the subdirectory (not the full repo), workspace resolution may fail.\"\n   190→\n   191→The concern is valid. Bun workspace resolution requires the workspace root's `package.json` to be accessible. If Vercel sets \"Root Directory\" to `docs/`, it typically checks out the full repo but sets the working directory to `docs/`. The `bun install` from `docs/` would need to traverse upward to find the root `package.json` with `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]`.\n   192→\n   193→The plan's fallback (change root directory to `/`, update buildCommand to `cd docs && bun run build`) is the safer path and should probably be listed as the **primary** approach rather than the fallback. The reason: Vercel's behavior with Bun workspaces and subdirectory roots is not well-documented, and the \"try it and fall back\" approach risks a failed deployment during what should be a clean migration. The plan should recommend doing the root-directory-to-`/` change upfront and treating the `docs/`-as-root approach as the optional optimization to try afterward.\n   194→\n   195→---\n   196→\n   197→## 11. The proposed smoke test (`monorepo-structure.test.ts`) follows the wrong test convention\n   198→\n   199→The plan (Section g) proposes an optional smoke test:\n   200→\n   201→```typescript\n   202→// tests/unit/monorepo-structure.test.ts\n   203→import { describe, test, expect } from \"bun:test\";\n   204→```\n   205→\n   206→Reading the existing unit test files (`json-schema.test.ts`, `utils.test.ts`, `config.test.ts`), they all import from `\"bun:test\"` and use `path.join(import.meta.dir, ...)` for path resolution. The proposed test uses `import.meta.dir` correctly.\n   207→\n   208→However, there is one convention issue: existing tests import from `\"bun:test\"` with destructured names. The proposed test also does this. That's correct.\n   209→\n   210→A more important issue: the plan says \"Only add if the team wants structural tests.\" In the test file list, there is no `monorepo-structure.test.ts`. The addition of this test is purely optional and clearly flagged as such. However, if it is added, it should follow the `cleanup-after-tests.ts` pattern the suite uses (the `package.json` `test:unit` script calls `bun tests/unit/cleanup-after-tests.ts` before and after the test suite). Since the proposed test only reads files and doesn't write anything, no cleanup is needed. This is fine.\n   211→\n   212→One actual gap: the test's `describe(\"monorepo structure\")` block is not scoped under `afterAll` cleanup, but since it only reads files, this is acceptable.\n   213→\n   214→---\n   215→\n   216→## 12. CI workflow: the plan says \"minimal changes needed\" but doesn't specify what changes ARE needed\n   217→\n   218→The plan (Section c code references) lists `ci.yml` as \"minimal changes needed\" and Phase 6 verifies everything works. But the plan never specifies what changes, if any, are needed to `ci.yml`. Reading the actual `ci.yml`:\n   219→\n   220→- The `setup-environment` action runs `bun install --frozen-lockfile` (line 34 of action.yml).\n   221→- After migration, the single root `bun.lock` will cover both workspaces. The `--frozen-lockfile` flag will require the new unified lockfile to be committed.\n   222→\n   223→The plan does address this in Section g (edge case 1):\n   224→\n   225→> \"`bun install --frozen-lockfile` in CI. The new unified `bun.lock` must be committed.\"\n   226→\n   227→But the plan does not say: **commit the new `bun.lock` as part of the migration commit**. This is an easy thing to forget. The migration commit checklist in Step 8.2 mentions adding `bun.lock` (\"Regenerate\" in the summary table), but it's not in the commit message template. The implementing agent should be explicitly told to stage and include the new `bun.lock` in the migration commit. Otherwise the first CI run after the PR is opened will fail on `--frozen-lockfile`.\n   228→\n   229→---\n   230→\n   231→## 13. The `standard-operating-procedures.md` line 40 edit: the plan's proposed wording is imprecise\n   232→\n   233→The plan (Step 5.2) proposes updating the SOP description of the `package.json` transformation at line 40. The current text is:\n   234→\n   235→> \"The `package.json` is transformed: name becomes `hankweave`, `publishConfig.access` becomes `public`, `homepage` points to the public repo, and `release:*` scripts are removed.\"\n   236→\n   237→The plan proposes adding \"and the `workspaces` field is deleted (since docs are not included in the public repo).\"\n   238→\n   239→Looking at the actual `transformPackageJson()` function in `sync-public.ts`, it also transforms `publishConfig` (sets `{ access: \"public\" }`, which replaces the existing `{ access: \"restricted\" }`). The SOP text says \"`publishConfig.access` becomes `public`\" — this is accurate and specific. The new sentence about `workspaces` being deleted is accurate and should be added. No substantive issue, just confirming the proposed wording is correct.\n   240→\n   241→---\n   242→\n   243→## 14. The plan doesn't address `bunfig.toml` at all — potential workspace-level concern\n   244→\n   245→The plan mentions `bunfig.toml` in its reference table (Section c) with \"no changes needed.\" The actual `bunfig.toml` is referenced but not read in this review session. However, `bunfig.toml` can contain workspace-level configuration in Bun (e.g., registry settings, test configurations). If the runtime's `bunfig.toml` has test-scoped settings, they would apply to all workspace members after migration.\n   246→\n   247→This is noted as an item to verify: the implementing agent should read `bunfig.toml` and confirm it has no settings that would unintentionally apply to the `docs/` workspace (e.g., custom registries, test patterns).\n   248→\n   249→---\n   250→\n   251→## 15. The plan identifies `generate-schemas.ts` line 76's `docsAvailable` as \"always true\" — but this is only true at runtime, not at commit time\n   252→\n   253→The plan says (Step 2.2):\n   254→\n   255→> \"The `docsAvailable` check at line 76 will now always evaluate to true since `docs/public/` is always present in the monorepo.\"\n   256→\n   257→This is true at runtime when running from the repo root after a checkout. But consider: `git sparse-checkout` or shallow clones could omit `docs/`. More relevantly, CI's checkout action (`actions/checkout@v4` with default settings) does a full checkout. So in practice, `docs/public/` will always be present in CI.\n   258→\n   259→The plan is correct in its conclusion but slightly overstates certainty. The comment it proposes — \"Always available in monorepo; guard kept for safety in case `docs/` is missing from checkout\" — is actually well-calibrated. Keep the guard.\n   260→\n   261→---\n   262→\n   263→## 16. Phase 8 step ordering: archiving the old repo should come last, but the plan conflates it with earlier steps\n   264→\n   265→The plan (Step 8.1) says to archive `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` \"after the monorepo migration is merged and Vercel is confirmed working.\" This is the right condition. However, the numbered phases suggest archiving is part of Phase 8 (\"Cleanup and commit\"), which follows Phase 7 (Vercel). The order is correct: merge first (Phase 8.2–8.3), confirm Vercel works (Phase 7), then archive (Step 8.1 conceptually moves to last). The numbering in the plan has Step 8.1 (archive) before 8.2 (commit) and 8.3 (PR), which is backwards — you can't archive the old repo before the PR is even opened.\n   266→\n   267→This is an ordering error in the plan. Step 8.1 (archive old repo) should be the *last* action, after the PR merges, CI passes, and Vercel deployment is confirmed. The plan text says the right thing (\"after the migration is merged and Vercel is confirmed working\") but the step numbering buries this in Phase 8 before the commit/PR steps.\n   268→\n   269→---\n   270→\n   271→## 17. Missing: what happens to `docs/.gitignore` from the original docs repo\n   272→\n   273→The plan (Step 1.1) says to copy the contents of `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` into `docs/`. The docs repo has a `.gitignore` at the `nextra-site/` level (inferred — not read directly, but standard Next.js projects have one). After migration, there may be a `docs/.gitignore` file copied over.\n   274→\n   275→The plan's Step 4.1 adds docs-specific gitignore entries to the root `.gitignore`. But if `docs/.gitignore` is carried over from the nextra-site, there may be duplicate or conflicting ignore rules. The plan does not address whether to keep or delete `docs/.gitignore`. Given that the root `.gitignore` already covers `node_modules/`, `.env`, `.DS_Store`, and `.next`, the docs-level `.gitignore` is redundant but harmless. The implementing agent should check what's in `docs/.gitignore` (from the nextra-site) and decide explicitly rather than silently including it.\n   276→\n   277→---\n   278→\n   279→## 18. Section b \"Assumptions\" — assumption #1 deserves more precision\n   280→\n   281→Assumption #1 states: \"The `hankweave-docs` repo does not have significant git history worth preserving.\" This is listed as an assumption without a clear source. The plan's own \"Decision 4\" (Section e) describes converting the existing hankweave repo in-place, and \"Section i\" provides the git subtree merge commands for history preservation. But the plan is ambiguous about what decision is actually made: is history preservation \"optional\" or \"skipped by default\"?\n   282→\n   283→For an implementing agent, \"optional\" means \"don't bother unless instructed.\" The plan should be explicit: **the default approach is a simple file copy (no history preservation), and the git subtree approach is documented only as an escape hatch.** The current phrasing in Step 1.1 says \"Alternatively, a simple file copy with a descriptive commit is simpler and the docs repo history remains available in the original repo if ever needed\" — which implies simple copy is the default. This is consistent, but the assumption at the top of the document should match this more explicitly.\n   284→\n   285→---\n   286→\n   287→## Summary of items the plan should update\n   288→\n   289→The following items are either errors, gaps, or ambiguities that the plan should address before handoff to an implementing agent:\n   290→\n   291→1. **Step 3.1**: Note that `STRIP_DIRS` is also iterated in `initialSync()` (lines 291–302), so that code path is covered automatically by the `STRIP_DIRS` addition. (Informational — no code change needed, but the plan should say this explicitly.)\n   292→\n   293→2. **Step 3.4**: The `allowFailure: true` + `2>/dev/null || true` combination is correct but the plan should verify whether `exec()` throws or suppresses when the path doesn't exist. (Already handled correctly, but worth noting explicitly.)\n   294→\n   295→3. **Step 5.2 / Step 8.2**: The new `bun.lock` must be staged and included in the migration commit. The commit message template (Step 8.2) should explicitly list `bun.lock` as a file to add.\n   296→\n   297→4. **Step 7.1 / CVE flag**: Change the \"implementing agent should confirm\" framing to \"this requires explicit human sign-off from whoever manages the docs Vercel deployment.\" The agent cannot make this call.\n   298→\n   299→5. **Step 7.2**: Recommend switching Vercel root directory to `/` and using `cd docs && bun run build` as the **primary** approach rather than the fallback. The subdirectory-as-root approach for Bun workspaces is uncertain.\n   300→\n   301→6. **Step 8.1 ordering**: Move Step 8.1 (archive old repo) to after the PR merges and Vercel deployment is confirmed — not as the first step in Phase 8.\n   302→\n   303→7. **Step 1.1 / docs `.gitignore`**: Explicitly address whether to keep or delete `docs/.gitignore` when copying `nextra-site/` contents into `docs/`. Recommend checking and deciding explicitly.\n   304→\n   305→8. **Step 2.2 comment text**: The proposed comment \"Always available in monorepo; guard kept for safety in case `docs/` is missing from checkout\" is good. Make it more precise: the guard checks `docs/public/` (the parent of `schemas/`), not `docs/` itself.\n   306→\n   307→9. **Section b Assumptions, #1**: Make explicit that the default approach is a simple file copy (no git history preservation), and that the git subtree method is documented as an optional escape hatch only.\n   308→\n   309→10. **Phase 3 general note on `bunfig.toml`**: Add a note to verify that `bunfig.toml` has no settings that would unintentionally apply to the docs workspace.\n   310→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a5d48287-fa06-41ff-8c53-7bb7d1a349eb","uuid":"7f46fbd3-c3ef-4c3d-b7c4-f9dd2e54e9b4","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/reviews/current/review.md","content":"# Review: Migrate Hankweave to Monorepo\n\nSource plan: `plan/merged/plan.md`\n\nThis review reads the plan with fresh eyes against the actual source code. Notes are grouped by the section of the plan they relate to. Each item describes what was observed and why it matters.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Line-number claims that are wrong or misleading\n\nThe plan references specific line numbers for several files. After reading the actual source, several are incorrect.\n\n**`sync-public.ts` — `STRIP_DIRS` is at line 28 (correct), but the plan's proposed change is incomplete.**\n\nThe plan (Step 3.1) says to add `\"docs\"` to `STRIP_DIRS`. Looking at the actual `prepareSnapshot()` function (`sync-public.ts:214`), the rsync excludes are built as:\n\n```typescript\nconst dirExcludes = [\".git\", ...STRIP_DIRS].map((p) => `--exclude='${p}/'`);\n```\n\nThis adds a trailing slash, making the rsync exclude `--exclude='docs/'`. That is correct for rsync behavior (excludes the directory but not a file named `docs`). No issue here, but the plan doesn't surface this detail. The implementing agent should understand that STRIP_DIRS entries become dir-style excludes with trailing slashes in the rsync call.\n\n**`sync-public.ts` — `transformPackageJson()` is at lines 173–196 (the plan says this).** After reading the code, the function runs at line 173 and ends at line 196. The plan's proposed diff (Step 3.2) would insert `delete pkg.workspaces;` after `pkg.homepage = ...`. This is correct. However, the plan quotes the full function in its proposed diff but omits the console.log lines at 189–195. The implementing agent should make a minimal targeted edit, not rewrite the entire function. This is a clarification, not a blocking issue.\n\n**`release.ts` — `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP` is at lines 54–60.** Confirmed correct.\n\n**`release.ts` — \"schema revert at around line 436.\"** The actual code at lines 424–445 shows the `git checkout -- schemas/` call is at line 436. This is accurate.\n\n**`release.ts` — \"release commit `git add` at line 510.\"** The actual code at lines 509–511 is:\n```typescript\nawait exec(\"git add package.json CHANGELOG.md schemas/\");\nawait exec(`git commit -m \"Release v${newVersion}\"`);\n```\nThe `git add` is indeed at line 510. Correct.\n\n**`test-sync-local.ts` — \"rsync at line 80.\"** The actual rsync call is at line 80:\n```\nrsync -av --exclude='.git' --exclude='node_modules' --exclude='intermediates' ${REPO_ROOT}/ private/\n```\nCorrect.\n\n**`test-sync-local.ts` — \"`shouldNotExist` at line 160.\"** The actual `shouldNotExist` array starts at line 160. Confirmed.\n\n**`generate-schemas.ts` — `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` at lines 24–32.** Confirmed correct. The `docsAvailable` check is at line 76. Confirmed correct.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The `generate-schemas.ts` `docsAvailable` guard: plan recommends keeping it but misses a real subtlety\n\nThe plan (Step 2.2) says:\n\n> \"Keep the check for defensive safety — it doesn't hurt.\"\n\nThe actual guard at line 76 is:\n\n```typescript\nconst docsAvailable = fs.existsSync(path.dirname(DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR));\n```\n\n`path.dirname(DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR)` in the post-migration world resolves to `docs/public`. This is the parent of the `schemas/` subdirectory inside `docs/public`. This means the guard checks whether `docs/public/` exists — not whether `docs/` exists. This is a fine distinction but matters: if someone were to run the script on a fresh checkout where `docs/public/` exists but `docs/public/schemas/` does not, the `mkdirSync` on line 78 creates it. That's correct behavior.\n\nThe plan's proposed comment update says: \"Always available in monorepo; guard kept for safety in case `docs/` is missing from checkout.\" This comment is slightly imprecise — the guard checks `docs/public/`, not `docs/`. The implementing agent should write the updated comment more precisely.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The `.gitignore` changes: the plan is partly wrong\n\nThe plan (Step 4.1) says:\n\n> \"The root `.gitignore` already has a generic `.next` and `out` entry (lines 141–142: `.next` and `out`), but since those are not scoped to `docs/`, they'd match those directories anywhere in the tree including at root.\"\n\nThis is accurate. What the plan then says — \"The `docs/`-prefixed versions are more explicit but the existing generic entries already cover the docs case\" — is also accurate, but the recommendation to only add `docs/public/_pagefind/` is incomplete.\n\nReading the actual `.gitignore`, the entry at line 147 is `dist`, not scoped to any subdirectory. The runtime's build outputs go into `releases/` (line 16 of the gitignore), not `dist/`. The `dist` entry in `.gitignore` therefore won't match the runtime build because the runtime uses `releases/` as its output directory. However, the `dist` entry *would* match `docs/dist/` if Next.js ever emits to `docs/dist/`. In practice, `next build` outputs to `.next/` and static export to `out/`. This entry is a non-issue for the actual migration.\n\nThe plan should clarify one item it misses: the docs workspace has a `bun.lock` that will be deleted (Step 1.3). That file won't be left around. But the `.next/` and `out` patterns at `.gitignore` lines 141–142 are *unscoped* and at root level. In gitignore syntax, a pattern without a leading `/` or `/` in the middle matches anywhere in the tree. So `.next` matches `docs/.next/` automatically. The plan is correct that the existing entries cover the docs case, but it should explicitly state this is because the patterns are tree-wide rather than hinting it's a coincidence.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The `sync-public.ts` `initialSync()` function also needs updating — the plan misses this\n\nThe plan (Phase 3) updates `STRIP_DIRS` to add `\"docs\"`. This correctly covers `prepareSnapshot()` and `snapshotSync()`. However, `initialSync()` at line 262 has its own explicit loop:\n\n```typescript\n// Remove internal files\nfor (const dir of STRIP_DIRS) {\n  const fullPath = join(publicDir, dir);\n  if (existsSync(fullPath)) {\n    await rm(fullPath, { recursive: true, force: true });\n  }\n}\n```\n\nSince `STRIP_DIRS` is a shared constant and `initialSync()` iterates the same constant, adding `\"docs\"` to `STRIP_DIRS` will cover `initialSync()` automatically. The plan does not explicitly mention this, which could confuse the implementing agent into thinking only `prepareSnapshot()` is affected. The plan should acknowledge that `initialSync()` is also covered by the `STRIP_DIRS` change because it uses the same constant.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The release script's schema handling: the plan's proposed code change has an issue\n\nThe plan (Step 3.4) proposes extending the validation-phase schema revert to:\n\n```typescript\nawait exec(\"git checkout -- schemas/ docs/public/schemas/ 2>/dev/null || true\", {\n  silent: true,\n  allowFailure: true,\n});\n```\n\nReading the actual code at lines 435–439:\n\n```typescript\nawait exec(\"git checkout -- schemas/ 2>/dev/null || true\", {\n  silent: true,\n  allowFailure: true,\n});\n```\n\nThe existing call uses `allowFailure: true`. The plan's proposed change correctly maintains `allowFailure: true`. No issue there. But there is a subtle problem: if `docs/public/schemas/` does not yet exist in the git index (fresh checkout, schemas not yet generated), `git checkout -- docs/public/schemas/` will fail with \"error: pathspec 'docs/public/schemas/' did not match any file(s) known to git.\" The `2>/dev/null || true` suffix in the shell command handles this at the shell level. However, the actual `exec()` helper checks exit code and only suppresses the throw if `allowFailure: true`. Since `allowFailure: true` is set, this is handled correctly.\n\nThe more important issue is different: the plan says \"without this, the validation phase leaves `docs/public/schemas/` in a modified-but-unstaged state.\" That is correct, but there is another consequence: `bun run tc` (invoked during validation at line 451) runs *after* schema generation at line 425. If schema generation modifies files in `docs/public/schemas/` and the revert at line 436 only covers `schemas/`, the `git status --porcelain` check at line 210 (in the next release run, not this one) would show uncommitted changes. The plan is right to fix this — just surfacing the precise reason.\n\n---\n\n## 6. The `release.ts` strip step: `docs/` vs `docs` in `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP`\n\nThe plan (Step 3.3) proposes adding `\"docs/\"` (with trailing slash) to `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP`. The existing entries use trailing slashes for directories (`\"intermediates/\"`, `\"external-docs/\"`) and no trailing slash for files (`\"CLAUDE.md\"`).\n\nThe merge conflict auto-resolution logic at line 380 checks `f.startsWith(s)`. Git reports paths like `docs/content/guides/getting-started.mdx`. The plan correctly notes that `\"docs/content/guides/getting-started.mdx\".startsWith(\"docs/\")` is `true`. This is correct.\n\nHowever, the strip execution at lines 592–607 uses:\n```typescript\nconst exists = await exec(`git ls-files ${file} | head -n 1`, { silent: true });\n```\nThis passes `\"docs/\"` (with trailing slash) as the git ls-files argument. `git ls-files docs/` correctly lists files under `docs/`, so trailing slash is fine for the ls-files call.\n\nThen at line 600: `await exec(\\`git rm -rf ${filesToRemove.join(\" \")}\\`)`. This passes `docs/` with trailing slash to `git rm -rf`. In git, `git rm -rf docs/` is equivalent to `git rm -rf docs` — both remove the directory recursively. No issue.\n\nThis is consistent with the existing entries. No action needed, but the plan could mention it.\n\n---\n\n## 7. The `test-sync-local.ts` update: plan is missing coverage for the `--initial` mode path\n\nThe plan (Step 3.5) adds `--exclude='docs'` to the `--use-head` rsync. However, looking at the actual code, the `--use-head` path is the only one that does an rsync from the live working directory. The other branch (lines 84–86) does a `git clone --branch v${version}`, which naturally excludes uncommitted `docs/` content (since `docs/` would be committed). The `--exclude='docs'` addition is therefore only needed in the `--use-head` path, which is what the plan proposes. This is correct.\n\nOne thing the plan does not address: in the `--use-head` path, after the rsync, the code does `git init && git add -A && git commit -m \"Test snapshot\"`. After migration, `docs/` exists at the repo root. If `--exclude='docs'` is added to rsync, then `docs/` won't be in the test snapshot — which is the desired behavior. This is consistent and correct. The plan is fine here.\n\n---\n\n## 8. The `package.json` `\"workspaces\"` field: unclear ordering instruction\n\nThe plan (Step 1.2) says to place `\"workspaces\"` \"after `\\\"engines\\\"` and before `\\\"scripts\\\"`.\" Looking at the actual `package.json`, the field order is:\n\n```\nname, version, description, author, keywords, homepage, type, publishConfig,\nbin, main, files, engines, scripts, devDependencies, peerDependencies, dependencies\n```\n\nSo `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` between `\"engines\"` and `\"scripts\"` is clear. This is correct and reasonable. No issue.\n\nHowever, the plan does not explicitly state what the minimal diff looks like. The plan only shows a fragment with `...` placeholders. For an implementing agent working without access to the full file, this could be ambiguous. The actual edit is just adding one line. It would be helpful for the plan to state this plainly: \"Add a single line `\\\"workspaces\\\": [\\\"docs\\\"],` between the closing `}` of `engines` and the opening `{` of `scripts`.\"\n\n---\n\n## 9. `vercel.json` CVE flag: this needs explicit team confirmation, not just a note\n\nThe plan (Step 7.1) mentions:\n\n> \"This env var flag is being carried over verbatim from the existing docs repo and is assumed to be intentional for the docs deployment. The implementing agent should confirm this is expected.\"\n\nThe actual `vercel.json` contains:\n\n```json\n\"build\": {\n  \"env\": {\n    \"DANGEROUSLY_DEPLOY_VULNERABLE_CVE_2026_0969\": \"1\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis is a Vercel-specific mechanism to acknowledge deploying with a known CVE. The plan correctly notes it and flags it for confirmation, but frames it as something the implementing agent should \"confirm.\" In practice, this flag was deliberately set in the existing docs repo — it was a conscious team decision. The implementing agent cannot \"confirm\" this on their own; it needs an explicit go-ahead from a human who owns the docs deployment. The plan should be clearer that this is a **human-approval gate**, not just a code check. The implementing agent should not proceed with Phase 7 without explicit sign-off from a team member who knows why this flag exists.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Phase 7 Vercel: the `installCommand` in `vercel.json` creates a workspace resolution problem\n\nThe plan (Step 7.2) identifies this correctly:\n\n> \"The `vercel.json` `installCommand` is `\\\"bun install\\\"`, which runs from the Vercel-configured root directory (`docs/`). Because `docs/` is a workspace member, Bun should look upward for the workspace root's `package.json` and install the full workspace dependency tree. However, if Vercel only mounts the subdirectory (not the full repo), workspace resolution may fail.\"\n\nThe concern is valid. Bun workspace resolution requires the workspace root's `package.json` to be accessible. If Vercel sets \"Root Directory\" to `docs/`, it typically checks out the full repo but sets the working directory to `docs/`. The `bun install` from `docs/` would need to traverse upward to find the root `package.json` with `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]`.\n\nThe plan's fallback (change root directory to `/`, update buildCommand to `cd docs && bun run build`) is the safer path and should probably be listed as the **primary** approach rather than the fallback. The reason: Vercel's behavior with Bun workspaces and subdirectory roots is not well-documented, and the \"try it and fall back\" approach risks a failed deployment during what should be a clean migration. The plan should recommend doing the root-directory-to-`/` change upfront and treating the `docs/`-as-root approach as the optional optimization to try afterward.\n\n---\n\n## 11. The proposed smoke test (`monorepo-structure.test.ts`) follows the wrong test convention\n\nThe plan (Section g) proposes an optional smoke test:\n\n```typescript\n// tests/unit/monorepo-structure.test.ts\nimport { describe, test, expect } from \"bun:test\";\n```\n\nReading the existing unit test files (`json-schema.test.ts`, `utils.test.ts`, `config.test.ts`), they all import from `\"bun:test\"` and use `path.join(import.meta.dir, ...)` for path resolution. The proposed test uses `import.meta.dir` correctly.\n\nHowever, there is one convention issue: existing tests import from `\"bun:test\"` with destructured names. The proposed test also does this. That's correct.\n\nA more important issue: the plan says \"Only add if the team wants structural tests.\" In the test file list, there is no `monorepo-structure.test.ts`. The addition of this test is purely optional and clearly flagged as such. However, if it is added, it should follow the `cleanup-after-tests.ts` pattern the suite uses (the `package.json` `test:unit` script calls `bun tests/unit/cleanup-after-tests.ts` before and after the test suite). Since the proposed test only reads files and doesn't write anything, no cleanup is needed. This is fine.\n\nOne actual gap: the test's `describe(\"monorepo structure\")` block is not scoped under `afterAll` cleanup, but since it only reads files, this is acceptable.\n\n---\n\n## 12. CI workflow: the plan says \"minimal changes needed\" but doesn't specify what changes ARE needed\n\nThe plan (Section c code references) lists `ci.yml` as \"minimal changes needed\" and Phase 6 verifies everything works. But the plan never specifies what changes, if any, are needed to `ci.yml`. Reading the actual `ci.yml`:\n\n- The `setup-environment` action runs `bun install --frozen-lockfile` (line 34 of action.yml).\n- After migration, the single root `bun.lock` will cover both workspaces. The `--frozen-lockfile` flag will require the new unified lockfile to be committed.\n\nThe plan does address this in Section g (edge case 1):\n\n> \"`bun install --frozen-lockfile` in CI. The new unified `bun.lock` must be committed.\"\n\nBut the plan does not say: **commit the new `bun.lock` as part of the migration commit**. This is an easy thing to forget. The migration commit checklist in Step 8.2 mentions adding `bun.lock` (\"Regenerate\" in the summary table), but it's not in the commit message template. The implementing agent should be explicitly told to stage and include the new `bun.lock` in the migration commit. Otherwise the first CI run after the PR is opened will fail on `--frozen-lockfile`.\n\n---\n\n## 13. The `standard-operating-procedures.md` line 40 edit: the plan's proposed wording is imprecise\n\nThe plan (Step 5.2) proposes updating the SOP description of the `package.json` transformation at line 40. The current text is:\n\n> \"The `package.json` is transformed: name becomes `hankweave`, `publishConfig.access` becomes `public`, `homepage` points to the public repo, and `release:*` scripts are removed.\"\n\nThe plan proposes adding \"and the `workspaces` field is deleted (since docs are not included in the public repo).\"\n\nLooking at the actual `transformPackageJson()` function in `sync-public.ts`, it also transforms `publishConfig` (sets `{ access: \"public\" }`, which replaces the existing `{ access: \"restricted\" }`). The SOP text says \"`publishConfig.access` becomes `public`\" — this is accurate and specific. The new sentence about `workspaces` being deleted is accurate and should be added. No substantive issue, just confirming the proposed wording is correct.\n\n---\n\n## 14. The plan doesn't address `bunfig.toml` at all — potential workspace-level concern\n\nThe plan mentions `bunfig.toml` in its reference table (Section c) with \"no changes needed.\" The actual `bunfig.toml` is referenced but not read in this review session. However, `bunfig.toml` can contain workspace-level configuration in Bun (e.g., registry settings, test configurations). If the runtime's `bunfig.toml` has test-scoped settings, they would apply to all workspace members after migration.\n\nThis is noted as an item to verify: the implementing agent should read `bunfig.toml` and confirm it has no settings that would unintentionally apply to the `docs/` workspace (e.g., custom registries, test patterns).\n\n---\n\n## 15. The plan identifies `generate-schemas.ts` line 76's `docsAvailable` as \"always true\" — but this is only true at runtime, not at commit time\n\nThe plan says (Step 2.2):\n\n> \"The `docsAvailable` check at line 76 will now always evaluate to true since `docs/public/` is always present in the monorepo.\"\n\nThis is true at runtime when running from the repo root after a checkout. But consider: `git sparse-checkout` or shallow clones could omit `docs/`. More relevantly, CI's checkout action (`actions/checkout@v4` with default settings) does a full checkout. So in practice, `docs/public/` will always be present in CI.\n\nThe plan is correct in its conclusion but slightly overstates certainty. The comment it proposes — \"Always available in monorepo; guard kept for safety in case `docs/` is missing from checkout\" — is actually well-calibrated. Keep the guard.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Phase 8 step ordering: archiving the old repo should come last, but the plan conflates it with earlier steps\n\nThe plan (Step 8.1) says to archive `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` \"after the monorepo migration is merged and Vercel is confirmed working.\" This is the right condition. However, the numbered phases suggest archiving is part of Phase 8 (\"Cleanup and commit\"), which follows Phase 7 (Vercel). The order is correct: merge first (Phase 8.2–8.3), confirm Vercel works (Phase 7), then archive (Step 8.1 conceptually moves to last). The numbering in the plan has Step 8.1 (archive) before 8.2 (commit) and 8.3 (PR), which is backwards — you can't archive the old repo before the PR is even opened.\n\nThis is an ordering error in the plan. Step 8.1 (archive old repo) should be the *last* action, after the PR merges, CI passes, and Vercel deployment is confirmed. The plan text says the right thing (\"after the migration is merged and Vercel is confirmed working\") but the step numbering buries this in Phase 8 before the commit/PR steps.\n\n---\n\n## 17. Missing: what happens to `docs/.gitignore` from the original docs repo\n\nThe plan (Step 1.1) says to copy the contents of `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` into `docs/`. The docs repo has a `.gitignore` at the `nextra-site/` level (inferred — not read directly, but standard Next.js projects have one). After migration, there may be a `docs/.gitignore` file copied over.\n\nThe plan's Step 4.1 adds docs-specific gitignore entries to the root `.gitignore`. But if `docs/.gitignore` is carried over from the nextra-site, there may be duplicate or conflicting ignore rules. The plan does not address whether to keep or delete `docs/.gitignore`. Given that the root `.gitignore` already covers `node_modules/`, `.env`, `.DS_Store`, and `.next`, the docs-level `.gitignore` is redundant but harmless. The implementing agent should check what's in `docs/.gitignore` (from the nextra-site) and decide explicitly rather than silently including it.\n\n---\n\n## 18. Section b \"Assumptions\" — assumption #1 deserves more precision\n\nAssumption #1 states: \"The `hankweave-docs` repo does not have significant git history worth preserving.\" This is listed as an assumption without a clear source. The plan's own \"Decision 4\" (Section e) describes converting the existing hankweave repo in-place, and \"Section i\" provides the git subtree merge commands for history preservation. But the plan is ambiguous about what decision is actually made: is history preservation \"optional\" or \"skipped by default\"?\n\nFor an implementing agent, \"optional\" means \"don't bother unless instructed.\" The plan should be explicit: **the default approach is a simple file copy (no history preservation), and the git subtree approach is documented only as an escape hatch.** The current phrasing in Step 1.1 says \"Alternatively, a simple file copy with a descriptive commit is simpler and the docs repo history remains available in the original repo if ever needed\" — which implies simple copy is the default. This is consistent, but the assumption at the top of the document should match this more explicitly.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of items the plan should update\n\nThe following items are either errors, gaps, or ambiguities that the plan should address before handoff to an implementing agent:\n\n1. **Step 3.1**: Note that `STRIP_DIRS` is also iterated in `initialSync()` (lines 291–302), so that code path is covered automatically by the `STRIP_DIRS` addition. (Informational — no code change needed, but the plan should say this explicitly.)\n\n2. **Step 3.4**: The `allowFailure: true` + `2>/dev/null || true` combination is correct but the plan should verify whether `exec()` throws or suppresses when the path doesn't exist. (Already handled correctly, but worth noting explicitly.)\n\n3. **Step 5.2 / Step 8.2**: The new `bun.lock` must be staged and included in the migration commit. The commit message template (Step 8.2) should explicitly list `bun.lock` as a file to add.\n\n4. **Step 7.1 / CVE flag**: Change the \"implementing agent should confirm\" framing to \"this requires explicit human sign-off from whoever manages the docs Vercel deployment.\" The agent cannot make this call.\n\n5. **Step 7.2**: Recommend switching Vercel root directory to `/` and using `cd docs && bun run build` as the **primary** approach rather than the fallback. The subdirectory-as-root approach for Bun workspaces is uncertain.\n\n6. **Step 8.1 ordering**: Move Step 8.1 (archive old repo) to after the PR merges and Vercel deployment is confirmed — not as the first step in Phase 8.\n\n7. **Step 1.1 / docs `.gitignore`**: Explicitly address whether to keep or delete `docs/.gitignore` when copying `nextra-site/` contents into `docs/`. Recommend checking and deciding explicitly.\n\n8. **Step 2.2 comment text**: The proposed comment \"Always available in monorepo; guard kept for safety in case `docs/` is missing from checkout\" is good. Make it more precise: the guard checks `docs/public/` (the parent of `schemas/`), not `docs/` itself.\n\n9. **Section b Assumptions, #1**: Make explicit that the default approach is a simple file copy (no git history preservation), and that the git subtree method is documented as an optional escape hatch only.\n\n10. **Phase 3 general note on `bunfig.toml`**: Add a note to verify that `bunfig.toml` has no settings that would unintentionally apply to the docs workspace.\n","numLines":310,"startLine":1,"totalLines":310}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:53:14.530Z"}
