{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AKGSkrDnKsH4iaWRk4sizg","type":"tool_result","content":"   360→    // Codes: UU (both modified), AA (both added), DD (both deleted),\n   361→    // DU/UD (modify/delete — e.g. file stripped on release but modified on develop)\n   362→    const mergeStatus = await exec(\"git status --porcelain\", { silent: true });\n   363→    const conflictPattern = /^(UU|AA|DD|DU|UD|AU|UA) /m;\n   364→    const hasConflicts = conflictPattern.test(mergeStatus);\n   365→\n   366→    // For modify/delete conflicts, check if ALL conflicting files are in the\n   367→    // strip list — if so, the merge step will auto-resolve them.\n   368→    let conflictsAreStrippable = false;\n   369→    if (hasConflicts) {\n   370→      const modifyDeletePattern = /^(DU|UD) (.+)$/gm;\n   371→      const allConflictPattern = /^(UU|AA|DD|DU|UD|AU|UA) (.+)$/gm;\n   372→\n   373→      const modifyDeleteFiles = [...mergeStatus.matchAll(modifyDeletePattern)].map((m) => m[2].trim());\n   374→      const allConflictFiles = [...mergeStatus.matchAll(allConflictPattern)].map((m) => m[2].trim());\n   375→\n   376→      if (\n   377→        allConflictFiles.length > 0 &&\n   378→        allConflictFiles.length === modifyDeleteFiles.length &&\n   379→        modifyDeleteFiles.every((f) =>\n   380→          INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP.some((s) => f === s || f.startsWith(s)),\n   381→        )\n   382→      ) {\n   383→        conflictsAreStrippable = true;\n   384→      }\n   385→    }\n   386→\n   387→    // Abort the merge\n   388→    await exec(\"git merge --abort 2>/dev/null || git reset --hard HEAD\", {\n   389→      silent: true,\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code be